Let's fix `next()` and `parse_mul`. Rewrite `next()` with a simple structure:

```rust
fn next(&mut self) -> Result<Option<(Tok, usize)>, Error> {
    self.skip_ws();
    let c = match self.ch(self.pos) {
        Some(c) => c,
        None => return Ok(None),
    };
    let start = self.pos;
    let b = c as u8;
    let single = match b {
        b'+' => Some(Tok::Add),
        b'-' => Some(Tok::Sub),
        b'*' => Some(Tok::Mul),
        b'/' => Some(Tok::Div),
        b'%' => Some(Tok::Mod),
        b'^' => Some(Tok::Pow),
        b'(' => Some(Tok::LParen),
        b')' => Some(Tok::RParen),
        b',' => Some(Tok::Comma),
        _ => None,
    };
    let t = match single {
        Some(t) => {
            self.pos += c.len_utf8();
            (t, start)
        }
        None if c.is_ascii_digit() || c == '.' => self.number(start)?,
        None if c.is_alphabetic() || c == '_' => self.ident(start),
        None => return Err(Error::UnexpectedChar(start)),
    };
    Ok(Some(t))
}
```

And parse_mul using a small enum:

```rust
#[derive(Clone, Copy)]
enum BinOp { Add, Sub, Mul, Div, Mod }
```

Actually, parse_expr can use the same pattern. Let's write it like this:

```rust
fn parse_mul(&mut self) -> Result<Node, Error> {
    let mut left = self.parse_unary()?;
    loop {
        let op = match self.peek()? {
            Some((Tok::Mul, _)) => Some(BinOp::Mul),
            Some((Tok::Div, _)) => Some(BinOp::Div),
            Some((Tok::Mod, _)) => Some(BinOp::Mod),
            _ => None,
        };
        let Some(op) = op else { break };
        self.bump()?;
        let r = self.parse_unary()?;
        left = match op {
            BinOp::Mul => Node::Mul(Box::new(left), Box::new(r)),
            BinOp::Div => Node::Div(Box::new(left), Box::new(r)),
            BinOp::Mod => Node::Mod(Box::new(left), Box::new(r)),
        };
    }
    Ok(left)
}
```

Similarly for parse_expr using BinOp::Add/Sub. Add Add and Sub to BinOp.

